\version "2.24.0"
% SECOND THEME (theme F2), bars 51-66, E flat minor, Poco meno mosso: the trumpet's soft melody owed since movement I
% (cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 1): trumpet 1 alone, undoubled, no timpani and no other brass in its register (Adler PDF 264, 267);
% the octave doubling (flute 1) is saved for its climax at 61-62 (Adler PDF 279-281). E flat (III) is a key neither earlier movement uses.
% Accompaniment: upper strings pizz. off the beat, cellos and basses pizz. on the beat, clarinets and bassoons sustained pp.
% The theme is heard first in minor and then in major (67), the reverse of movement I's second theme (cyclic-notes).

% c032 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 283, Ex. 17-24: the Phrygian IV6-V): 57 Cl 2 E flat3 on beat 3 (was A flat3: A flat-B flat under the solo's E flat-F, parallel 5ths into V at 58).
% c046 (Adler PDF 292, 321: slurred notes are one breath; with no rest between the 4-bar slurs the player must cut a note): breath marks after 54, 58, 62 (and 174, 178, 182 in recS with the cor anglais)
% c048 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 54-55: the accented dissonance carries the stress, its resolution is lighter): 62 the diminuendo starts on beat 2, after the peak B flat 5, and covers the falling group (was on beat 4 only); same at 182 in recS
sOneTptOne = { bes'2.(\p^\markup \italic "dolce cantabile" ges'4 | f'4 es'4 ges'4 bes'4 | f''2 es''4 des''4 | ces''2 bes'2) \breathe | aes'2.( ces''4 | bes'4 aes'4 ges'4 f'4 | ges'2 es'2 | f'1) \breathe | bes'2.( ges'4 | f'4 es'4 ges'4 bes'4\< | es''2. f''4 | bes''4\mf aes''4\> ges''4 f''4) \breathe | es''2.(\p des''4 | ces''4 bes'4 aes'4 ges'4 | f'2 d'2 | es'1) | }
% c041 (melody; Adler ch. 7, PDF 185-186: the flute's top fourth is "a bit shrill", hard to play softly, best approached by scale): 61-63 Fl 1 doubles the soft trumpet at the unison (F5-B flat5) instead of the octave (leap F6-B flat6 at p-mf above a p solo).
% c145 (engraving; Adler ch. 18, PDF 295: all instruments clearly marked in all systems; PDF 301, Ex. XVIII-10a: "1." over the playing part of a shared staff): 61 Fl 1 "1." (score only); the part combiner printed it last at 43, 16 bars and a page earlier.
sOneFluteOne = { R1*10 | r2 r4 \once \override Staff.Hairpin.springs-and-rods = #ly:spanner::set-spacing-rods \once \override Staff.Hairpin.minimum-length = #7 f''4(\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "1." | bes''4\mf aes''4\> ges''4 f''4) | es''2.(\p des''4) | R1*3 | }
% c147 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 76-77: parallel fifths between any two voices; Rimsky PDF 112: in a chord the octaves strengthen one another, so inner-voice fifths in doubled pizz. chords are heard): 62 beat 4 Vln II D4-A flat4 (was D4-F4): its G flat4-F4 made fifths with the violas' C flat4-B flat3 (62.2 -> 62.4) under the solo's climax; the seventh A flat4 falls to G flat4 at 63.
sOneVlnTwo = { r4 <es' ges'>4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 <es' ges'>4 | r4 <f' aes'>4 r4 <es' ges'>4 | r4 <es' aes'>4 r4 <es' aes'>4 | r4 <es' aes'>4 r4 <es' ges'>4 | r4 <es' f'>4 r4 <es' f'>4 | r4 <d' f'>4 r4 <d' f'>4 | r4 <es' ges'>4 r4 <es' aes'>4 | r4 <d' f'>4 r4 <d' f'>4 | r4 <es' ges'>4 r4 <es' ges'>4 | r4 <f' aes'>4 r4 <es' ges'>4 | r4 <es' ges'>4 r4 <es' f'>4 | r4 <es' ges'>4 r4 <d' aes'>4 | r4 <es' ges'>4 r4 <es' ges'>4 | r4 <es' aes'>4 r4 <d' bes'>4 | r4 <d' f'>4 r4 <d' f'>4 | r4 <es' ges'>4 r2 | }
sOneVla = { r4 <ges bes>4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz. div." r4 <ges bes>4 | r4 <f aes>4 r4 <ges bes>4 | r4 <aes ces'>4 r4 <aes ces'>4 | r4 <aes ces'>4 r4 <ges bes>4 | r4 <f aes>4 r4 <f aes>4 | r4 <f bes>4 r4 <f aes>4 | r4 <ges bes>4 r4 <aes es'>4 | r4 <f bes>4 r4 <f aes>4 | r4 <ges bes>4 r4 <ges bes>4 | r4 <f aes>4 r4 <ges bes>4 | r4 <ges bes>4 r4 <f aes>4 | r4 <aes ces'>4 r4 <f bes>4 | r4 <ges es'>4 r4 <ges es'>4 | r4 <aes ces'>4 r4 <f aes>4 | r4 <f bes>4 r4 <f aes>4 | r4 <ges bes>4 r2 | }
sOneVc = { es4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | d4 r4 es4 r4 | ces4 r4 r2 | aes,4 r4 ges,4 r4 | f,4 r4 r2 | bes,4 r4 r2 | es4 r4 ces4 r4 | bes,4 r4 r2 | ges,4 r4 r2 | d4 r4 es4 r4 | ges,4 r4 aes,4 r4 | aes,4 r4 bes,4 r4 | ces4 r4 r2 | aes,4 r4 bes,4 r4 | bes,4 r4 r2 | es4 r4 r2 | }
sOneCb = { es,4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | d,4 r4 es,4 r4 | ces,4 r4 r2 | aes,,4 r4 ges,,4 r4 | f,,4 r4 r2 | bes,,4 r4 r2 | es,4 r4 ces,4 r4 | bes,,4 r4 r2 | ges,,4 r4 r2 | d,4 r4 es,4 r4 | ges,,4 r4 aes,,4 r4 | aes,,4 r4 bes,,4 r4 | ces,4 r4 r2 | aes,,4 r4 bes,,4 r4 | bes,,4 r4 r2 | es,4 r4 r2 | }
% c093 (A&S Unit 14, PDF 214 and 228 point 2: the leading tone is not doubled; PDF 211, Ex. 14-3: V6/5 over the neighbour bass): 52 and 60 the neighbour chord over the bass D is V6/5 (Cl 1 B flat3, Vln II F4-A flat4); Cl 1 and Vln II had D4 over the bass D and rose to E flat with it in octaves (fixed at 65 by c092; the recapitulation 172, 180 already has the leading tone in the bass only).
% c093 (A&S PDF 216 and 220: the dominant prepares the new tonic; V7's drive to I): 51 Bn 1 G flat3-E flat3, so the 7th A flat3 it held through 43-50 falls by step into the second theme.
% c141 (Sebesky ch. 6, PDF 207-208, Ex. 271: an inner voice moving inside the chord carries one bar into the next; PDF 212, Ex. 280 and ch. 7 PDF 213, Ex. 286: the background moves where the solo holds): 58 Cl 2 B flat3-A flat3-F3-A flat3 in quarters under the trumpet's held F4 (was B flat3-A flat3 in halves); the seventh A flat falls to G flat at 59, as the counter-line does at 74 in the major statement (c051). Same at 178 in recS (G3-F3-D3-F3). Scoring and dynamics of the accompaniment unchanged.
% c145 (Adler ch. 18, PDF 301: proofread score and parts for consistency): 58 breath for Cl 2 and Bn 1 at the end of the bar, as at 178 (c126); both play 51-62 without a rest.
% c147 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 106: wood-wind joined to a brass line 'reinforces the brass, softens it and reduces its characteristic qualities'): 57 Cl 2 B flat3-E flat3 (was G flat3-E flat3, the trumpet's G flat4-E flat4 an octave lower: the undoubled solo was doubled for one bar, in parallel octaves). The recapitulation (177, Cl 2 E flat3-C4) never had it.
% c188 (Adler ch. 10, PDF 309, 321; Ex. X-16 'sehr weich', PDF 306): the sustained pp clarinet/bassoon chords under the trumpet solo tie their common tones (52-54, 60-62, 63-64, 65-66; Cl 2 53-54; Bn 1 60-62, 63-65) instead of re-attacking them each bar; re-attacks stay at 55, 59 and 63, where the solo breathes. Notes and dynamics unchanged.
% c205 (Gould PDF 50: when the other part lies on ledger lines, the crotchet rest of the upper part may return to the centre of the stave; PDF 51: a rest stays at the level of the surrounding pitches):
% 58.4 Cl 1's rest was drawn across the top line, three spaces above Cl 1's D4 and Cl 2's A flat3 (both below the stave); moved two spaces down to the centre.
% c216 (playability; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 211, Ex. VII-57: the clarinet, better than any other woodwind, can start a tone "from almost complete silence without articulation", the niente attack):
%   51 Cl 1-2 begin their pp chord dal niente, so the trumpet's first note (and the pizz. bass) is the only attack on the downbeat of the solo; level pp unchanged. The text prints once on the shared staff (Cl 2's copy is part-only).
sOneClOne = { es'1\pp^\markup \italic "dal niente" | bes2 es'2~ | es'1~ | es'1 | es'1 | d'1 | es'1 | d'2. \tag #'score { \once \override Rest.extra-offset = #'(0 . -2) } r4 | es'1 | bes2 es'2~ | es'1~ | es'2 d'2 | es'1~ | es'2 d'2 | bes1~ | bes1 | }
sOneClTwo = { ges1\pp-\tag #'part ^\markup \italic "dal niente" | aes2 ges2 | ces'1~ | ces'2 ges2 | aes1 | bes2 aes2 | bes2 es2 | bes4( aes4 f4 aes4) \breathe | bes2 ges2 | aes2 ges2 | ges2 ces'2~ | ces'2 bes4 r4 | ges1 | aes1 | bes2 aes2 | ges1 | }
% c092 (A&S PDF 76, PDF 83): 65 Bn 1 D3-B flat2, joining the cellos' B flat2 (was D3 held: it doubled the solo's leading tone D4 and both rose to E flat in octaves at 66).
% c094 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 97 remark 4: a three-part wind basis over a separate bass should form an independent whole, without open fourths and fifths; PDF 92: two of one timbre and a third of another): 53 Bn 1 A flat3 and Cl 2 C flat4 (held to 54 beat 3), so the trio is A flat3-C flat4-E flat4 (was E flat3-A flat3-E flat4; C flat in Cl 1 would move E flat-C flat in octaves with the pizz. bass); 56 Bn 1 F3 (trio F3-B flat3-D4, was D3-B flat3-D4 with the leading tone doubled).
% c144 (Adler ch. 8, PDF 257: pitches assigned so as not to upset the balance of the chord; A&S PDF 76, 83, as c092 at 65): 58 Bn 1 D3-F3 (was D3 held): its D3 and Cl 1's D4 (with Vln II's pizz. D4) both rose to E flat at 59 in octaves; F3 falls to E flat3 by step.
sOneBsnOne = { ges2\pp es2 | d2 es2 | aes1 | es1 | es1 | f1 | es1 | d2 f2 \breathe | es1 | d2 es2~ | es1~ | es2 bes,4 r4 | es1~ | es2 d2~ | d2 bes,2 | es1 | }
% c075 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues in long rests, audible and obvious, in the reading key; Example 4, PDF 10): part-only cue, tag 'part (score.ly removes it from the score); the end of the trumpet solo (63-66) before the harp's first entry at 67, after 66 bars
sOneHarpR = { R1*12 | \tag #'score { R1*4 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "tptOne" #DOWN { R1*4^\markup \tiny "Tpt. 1" } } | }
